WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.900
- Remember that scene in Armageddon where

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:03.630
in exchange for landing on a meteorite

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.250
and planting a nuclear bomb on it,

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.260
all the guys want in exchange
is to never pay taxes again?

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.703
God that was a good script.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:13.760
The fight over Donald Trump's tax returns

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.900
is a battle between an unstoppable force,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.600
like a meteorite, and an immovable object.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.410
The unstoppable force in this case

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.190
is the House Democratic Majority,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.780
which, since they came into power

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.630
in the 2018 midterm elections,

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.450
have made getting a look at Trump's

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.760
past tax returns a major priority.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.220
The immovable object is Trump himself.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.560
Ever since he became a
candidate for president,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.770
way back in the summer of 2015,

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.770
he has refused to release even
a single year of tax returns.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.830
- I'm releasing when we're
finished with the audit.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.450
Hopefully it's gonna
be before the election.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.400
- He's got a 12,000 page tax
return that's under audit.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.370
There's nothing there.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:53.670
- You know the only one that cares about

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.370
my tax returns are the reporters, okay?

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.311
- [Reporter] You don't think the American

00:00:56.311 --> 00:00:57.144
public is concerned about it?

00:00:57.144 --> 00:00:59.003
- No I don't think so, I won.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.860
- For the first two years
of Trump's presidency,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.180
nothing really moved on
the tax returns front.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.650
Sure, every April 15th
Democrats made a big stink

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.320
about the fact that Trump
is the first president

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.870
in the modern era, since
Richard Nixon effectively,

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.390
not to release any tax returns.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.750
But there was no way they could attempt

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.040
to force Trump's hand since Republicans

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.685
controlled the House and the Senate

00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:22.120
and had a total of zero
interest in spending time

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.880
on getting a look at
President Trump's taxes.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:29.520
That all changed in a big
way on November 6th, 2018,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.170
when democrats picked up 40
republican held house seats,

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.400
and, in so doing, retook the majority

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.310
they had lost eight years prior.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:37.820
Now, why did it change?

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.800
Well, because of a provision of tax

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.340
law from way back in 1924.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.850
I'm sure you know about
this, but known as code 6103,

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.190
the little known piece of the law

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.740
allows the Chairman of the
House Ways and Means Committee

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.840
to request any individual's tax return.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.927
And according to the code,
the Treasury Secretary quote,

00:01:55.927 --> 00:01:57.207
"shall furnish such committee

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:59.247
"with any return or return information

00:01:59.247 --> 00:02:02.294
"specified in such request," end quote.

00:02:02.294 --> 00:02:03.480
A lot of suches.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.110
Which is exactly what
Ways and Means Committee

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.540
Chairman Richard Neal of Massachusetts is

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.300
in the process right now of doing.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.937
Quote, "I think the President
has an opportunity here

00:02:11.937 --> 00:02:14.927
"to defuse this and just release the forms

00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:18.310
"as every other candidate
for President has done,"

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.527
Neal said shortly after the 2018 election.

00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:25.157
"Even if the request is made,
I don't expect that afternoon

00:02:25.157 --> 00:02:26.877
"we're going to see those tax forms.

00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:29.047
"So I think we'll do what we have to do,

00:02:29.047 --> 00:02:30.917
"and then see where the road takes us

00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.832
"and the path that we travel."

00:02:33.832 --> 00:02:34.930
That's deep.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.780
It's pretty clear that
Trump isn't going to just,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.610
you know, turn over his tax returns.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.760
He's spent three years making

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.530
a variety of excuses for why he can't,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.400
ranging from that he's under audit,

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.570
to that his taxes are just too complex

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.010
for the average person to understand,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.030
to the idea that regular people,
ah, they just don't care.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.320
Why has Trump fought so hard against

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.070
what had become standard
disclosure practice

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.840
for presidential candidates
and presidents in the past?

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.230
I don't know, but here's what I do know:

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.800
early on his campaign, Trump decided that

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.330
whatever was in his returns was way worse

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.310
than the negative press he might endure

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.490
for not releasing his returns.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:14.866
This is a hill Trump
seems willing to die on.

00:03:14.866 --> 00:03:17.420
Now it's no surprise, given all
that water under the bridge,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.630
that the Trump White House
doesn't exactly see the things

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.640
the way Neal and House Democrats do

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.640
when it comes to Trump's returns.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.090
The Treasury Department
has, at least publicly,

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.547
been very tight lipped about
the whole thing, quote,

00:03:29.547 --> 00:03:32.160
"Secretary Mnuchin will review any request

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.760
with the Treasury General
Counsel for legality,"

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.670
a spokeswoman told Politico in February.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.330
But behind the scenes,
there's an active effort

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:41.560
at both the Treasury Department

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.340
and among Republicans
in the House and Senate

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.490
to mount both a legal
and a political defense

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.390
against the Democratic request

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.730
to get a look at Trump's taxes.

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.210
And the Republican argument goes

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:54.340
a little something like this:

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.220
Democrats' desire to view Trump's taxes

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.080
have nothing to do with
the public interest

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.112
and absolutely everything to do

00:04:00.112 --> 00:04:03.010
with political considerations.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:04.730
Quote, "where does it end?

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.350
What about the tax returns of the speaker?

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:07.510
Political donors?

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:09.580
There is no end in sight."

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.260
that's Republican Pennsylvania
Congressman Mike Kelly,

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.870
who's a member of the
Ways and Means Committee.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:15.597
He added this: quote, "Every single

00:04:15.597 --> 00:04:18.860
"American has the right to privacy."

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.300
Republicans believe that if Trump's

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.600
tax returns are turned over to Neal,

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.390
they would inevitably be leaked

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.263
to the public in very short order,

00:04:26.263 --> 00:04:29.140
and that such a leak
would be a violation of,

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.870
among other things,
Trump's right to privacy.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:34.480
So, where do we go from here?

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.480
Well, Neal must first
formally make the request.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:39.640
And to be honest, he's kinda
taking his time with it,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.450
making sure that there are
no obvious legal loopholes

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.130
that either the Treasury Department

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.040
or Trump's attorneys can cite to allow

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.800
the President to wiggle out of disclosure.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.190
Quote, "We are doing our due diligence,"

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.580
Neal told CNN's Lauren Fox in early March.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.500
Quote, "I don't have a
timeline," end quote.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:58.710
That clears things up.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:00.500
When Neal does formally make his request,

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:02.010
it's very likely that Mnunchin

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.780
will either ignore it completely

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.550
or refuse to turn the records over.

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:09.380
At which point, Neal will
subpoena the returns.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.251
And the Trump administration will,

00:05:11.251 --> 00:05:13.901
I assume, fight that subpoena.

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:16.110
So fast forward, fast
forward, fast forward,

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.190
long story short, the
case will almost certainly

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:20.160
find its way to the Supreme Court,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.970
since neither side seems willing

00:05:21.970 --> 00:05:24.740
to bend an inch on this issue.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.330
Now if this fight lands in the court,

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.430
it could well happen in the midst

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.590
of a very contentious 2020 campaign

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:34.270
between Trump and the eventual

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.820
democratic presidential nominee.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.960
If that comes to pass, you can be sure

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.473
it would be a major issue
in that very campaign.

00:05:42.473 --> 00:05:44.350
All of which would put the Supreme Court

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.010
in a place it really hates being:

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:51.020
at the epicenter of a
white hot political debate.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.610
And that is The Point.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.060
We make new Point episodes
every Tuesday and Thursday.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.660
Make sure to check them all out.

